<LegalLayout
  title="Commercial License Agreement"
  description="The paid Merkleye licence: what counts as a domain for metering, term and renewal, trials, support, and the AGPL exception."
  updated="[EFFECTIVE DATE]"
>
  <p>
    This agreement is between <span class="tbd">[ENTITY LEGAL NAME]</span>
    (&ldquo;Merkleye&rdquo;) and the organisation named on the order form
    (&ldquo;Customer&rdquo;). It applies to any deployment covered by a paid
    licence file. Where it conflicts with the
    <a href="/legal/terms/">Terms of Service</a>, this agreement governs.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What you are buying</h2>
  <p>
    The Merkleye software itself is licensed under AGPL-3.0 and remains so. A
    commercial licence buys three separate things:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Entitlement.</strong> The right to monitor more than one
      registrable domain and to enable the features designated commercial in
      the documentation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Support and assurance</strong> at the level on the order form —
      response targets, signed and attested release artefacts, security
      advisories, and a named contact at the higher tiers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Optionally, an AGPL exception.</strong> Where the order form says
      so, Customer receives the same software under the alternative proprietary
      terms in section 8, instead of the AGPL.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>2. Metering — what a domain is</h2>
  <p>
    The licence file carries a maximum number of domains. That number counts
    <strong>registrable domains explicitly configured for monitoring</strong> —
    each unique effective top-level domain plus one label, as determined by the
    Public Suffix List. <code>example.com</code> and <code>example.co.uk</code>
    are two domains.
  </p>
  <p>The following <strong>never</strong> count against the limit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Generated lookalike permutations.</strong> A monitored domain
      with five thousand dnstwist variants counts as one. Permutations are
      unlimited at every tier, including the free edition.
    </li>
    <li>
      Subdomains and subject alternative names discovered under a monitored
      domain. Observing <code>vpn.example.com</code> consumes no additional
      entitlement.
    </li>
    <li>
      Certificates, observations, matches, or findings of any volume.
    </li>
    <li>Users, seats, API tokens, or notification channels.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Exceeding the limit does not disable monitoring of domains already
    configured. Merkleye warns, continues watching everything already
    configured, and refuses to add new domains until the entitlement is
    increased. Contact us and we will issue an updated licence file, prorated
    to your existing term.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Term, renewal, and fees</h2>
  <p>
    The initial term is stated on the order form, is normally twelve months,
    and is billed annually in advance. Fees are exclusive of tax. Invoices are
    due within <span class="tbd">[30]</span> days.
  </p>
  <p>
    Terms renew only if both parties agree; <strong>this agreement does not
    auto-renew</strong>. Because licensing is offline, we will contact you
    before expiry. Renewal pricing will not increase by more than
    <span class="tbd">[7%]</span> over the prior term unless your entitlement
    changes.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Evaluation licences</h2>
  <p>
    Evaluation licences are granted for 30 days, permit up to 250 domains,
    enable every feature, and carry no support commitment, no warranty, and no
    indemnity. They are for genuine evaluation, one per organisation, and may
    not be chained to avoid paying for production use. At expiry the deployment
    degrades to the free edition&rsquo;s limits and continues operating.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Support</h2>
  <p>
    We will use commercially reasonable efforts to meet the response targets on
    the order form. Targets are for
    <strong>first meaningful response, not resolution</strong>. Support covers
    the current release and the previous minor release, in an unmodified
    deployment.
  </p>
  <p>Out of scope: bugs that only appear in a modified build, third-party
    software we do not distribute, your infrastructure, and general security
    consulting.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Restrictions</h2>
  <p>Customer may not:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      share, resell, or sublicense a licence file, or use one across legal
      entities not named on the order form, except under a signed OEM or MSSP
      agreement;
    </li>
    <li>
      misrepresent the number of monitored domains in order to reduce fees;
    </li>
    <li>use the Merkleye name or marks except as the
      <a href="/legal/trademark/">Trademark Policy</a> permits.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Nothing in this section restricts any right granted by the AGPL to
    Customer&rsquo;s own copy of the software.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Verification</h2>
  <p>
    Merkleye performs no remote licence checks and collects no usage data by
    default, so verification is by self-certification: on written request, no
    more than once a year, Customer will confirm the number of registrable
    domains configured. There is no audit right, no inspection right, and no
    telemetry requirement.
  </p>

  <h2>8. AGPL exception (where purchased)</h2>
  <p>
    Where the order form includes it, Merkleye grants Customer a
    non-exclusive, non-transferable licence to use, modify, and internally
    distribute the software under proprietary terms in place of AGPL-3.0, for
    the term. Under this exception Customer has no obligation to disclose
    modifications to any party. The exception does not permit redistribution to
    third parties or offering the software as a service, which require a
    separate OEM agreement.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Warranties</h2>
  <p>
    We warrant that the software will perform materially as documented for
    <span class="tbd">[90]</span> days, and that we have the right to grant
    this licence. Your exclusive remedy for breach is repair, replacement, or a
    prorated refund.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>We do not warrant that Merkleye will detect any particular
    certificate, lookalike domain, or attack.</strong> Certificate Transparency
    covers publicly-trusted certificate authorities only; certificates from an
    internal PKI, self-signed certificates, and anything issued outside the
    browser root programmes will never appear. Detection also depends on
    third-party log operators we do not control. Except as stated here, the
    software is provided as is.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Indemnity</h2>
  <p>
    We will defend Customer against a third-party claim that unmodified
    Merkleye infringes that party&rsquo;s intellectual property, and pay damages
    finally awarded, provided Customer notifies us promptly and lets us control
    the defence. This does not apply to modified builds, combinations with
    other software, or use in breach of this agreement.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Limitation of liability</h2>
  <p>
    Neither party is liable for indirect, incidental, special, consequential,
    or punitive damages, or for lost profits or lost data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each party&rsquo;s total aggregate liability is capped at the fees paid or
    payable in the <span class="tbd">[12]</span> months before the claim.
    Excluded from the cap: Customer&rsquo;s payment obligations, either
    party&rsquo;s breach of confidentiality, our indemnity in section 10, and
    liability that cannot lawfully be limited.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Confidentiality</h2>
  <p>
    Each party will protect the other&rsquo;s non-public information with at
    least reasonable care and use it only to perform this agreement. The
    obligation survives three years past termination.
  </p>

  <h2>13. Data protection</h2>
  <p>
    Merkleye is self-hosted, so in the ordinary case we process none of
    Customer&rsquo;s monitoring data and are not a processor of it. Where we do
    process personal data — account records, and support diagnostics if
    Customer enables them — the <a href="/legal/dpa/">Data Processing
    Addendum</a> applies and is incorporated here.
  </p>

  <h2>14. Termination</h2>
  <p>
    Either party may terminate for material breach not cured within
    <span class="tbd">[30]</span> days of written notice. On termination
    Customer&rsquo;s entitlement ends; because the software is AGPL-licensed,
    Customer&rsquo;s rights in the code itself continue under that licence, and
    the deployment continues to operate at the free edition&rsquo;s limits.
    Fees already paid are non-refundable except where section 9 applies.
  </p>

  <h2>15. General</h2>
  <p>
    Governing law and venue are as stated in the
    <a href="/legal/terms/">Terms of Service</a>. Neither party may assign
    without consent, except to a successor of substantially all its business.
    This agreement, the order form, and the documents it incorporates are the
    entire agreement; Customer&rsquo;s purchase-order terms have no effect.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
